**Samsung Galaxy phones can't show you who tried to unlock them.** One UI counts wrong PIN, pattern and password guesses only to lock the phone for longer and longer. It doesn't keep a list you can check, and it never takes a photo. To see who it was, install an app that reacts to failed unlocks, like [CrookCatcher](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.harteg.crookcatcher&referrer=utm_source%3Dwebsite%26utm_medium%3Dguide%26utm_campaign%3Dsamsung-who-tried-to-unlock). It takes a photo with the front camera after a wrong PIN and emails it to you with the location.

## What your Samsung does after a wrong PIN

Samsung's newest lock screen policy (One UI 9) makes the person wait longer after each wrong guess:

| Wrong guesses | Wait before the next try |
| --- | --- |
| 5 | 1 minute |
| 6 | 5 minutes |
| 7 | 15 minutes |
| 8 | 30 minutes |
| 9 | 90 minutes |
| 10 | 4 hours |
| 11 | 12 hours |
| 12 | 24 hours |
| 13 | Phone is locked permanently |

After the 13th wrong guess, the phone can only be used again after a factory reset, which erases everything on it. Samsung can't recover your PIN for you.

A few details from Samsung:

- **This applies to phones that come with One UI 9.** Phones updated to One UI 9 keep their old behavior until they're factory reset.
- **Repeating the same wrong entry doesn't count again.** Identical wrong attempts in a row are skipped, so a child tapping the same code won't lock you out.
- **The lock screen warns you** how many attempts are left once someone gets close to the limit.

That warning, or a "Try again in…" message, is the only sign you'll see that someone tried. It doesn't say who, when, or how many times.

## Samsung settings worth turning on

These don't show you who tried, but they make your phone much harder to get into or take away.

### Theft protection

**Go to:**
`Settings > Security and privacy > Lost device protection > Theft protection`

- **Identity check:** away from places you've marked as safe (like home), changing sensitive security settings needs your fingerprint or face, not just your PIN.
- **Security delay:** part of Identity check. If someone tries to change your biometrics, the change only takes effect after an hour, which gives you time to lock the phone remotely.
- **Theft detection lock:** locks the screen if the phone detects it's been snatched.
- **Offline device lock:** locks the screen if a thief keeps the phone disconnected.

### Secure lock settings

**Go to:**
`Settings > Lock screen > Secure lock settings` (enter your PIN)

- **Lock network and security:** keeps network and security functions locked while the phone is locked, so a thief can't simply cut the connection or power it off from the lock screen. More on this in [how to stop thieves from turning off your phone](/blog/block-power-menu).
- **Auto factory reset:** erases the phone after too many wrong unlock attempts (Samsung's support page gives 15). Think twice before turning this on if children use your phone. It also erases any intruder photos stored on the phone, which is one more reason to have them emailed to you.

## What Samsung can't tell you

- **Who tried.** No photo of the person.
- **When it happened**, or how many times.
- **Where the phone was** at the time.
- **An alert while it happens.**

## How to get a photo of whoever tries to unlock your Samsung

CrookCatcher fills exactly that gap. When someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, it takes a photo with the front camera, records the location and an approximate address, and emails both to you. Every attempt is also saved in the app with its time.

