**Set it up on your Galaxy:**

1. **Install CrookCatcher** from <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.harteg.crookcatcher&referrer=utm_source%3Dwebsite%26utm_medium%3Dguide%26utm_campaign%3Dsamsung-who-tried-to-unlock" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Play</a>. There's no account to create.
2. **Allow Device admin.** This Android permission is how the app is told that an unlock attempt failed. CrookCatcher uses it only for that.
3. **Allow camera and location access.**
4. **Connect a Gmail account** for the alert emails. CrookCatcher can only *send* emails from it, not read them.
5. **Keep it awake.** Samsung can put apps to sleep to save battery, which can stop the alerts. Add CrookCatcher to **Never sleeping apps**: `Settings > Battery > Background usage limits > Never sleeping apps` (on older models: `Settings > Battery and device care > Battery > Background usage limits`).
6. **Test it.** Lock your phone, enter a wrong PIN on purpose, then unlock it. Your own photo should appear in the app and your inbox.

You choose how many wrong attempts it takes before CrookCatcher acts. Since 2014, CrookCatcher has been downloaded more than 12 million times, and it takes around 1.2 million intruder photos a day.

